# S234: S095's mate row re-mined on the candidate tree, 2026-09-24.
#
# WHY. S234 decides reverse futility on the node's estimate -- the table's own
# score where the entry's bound certifies the direction it has moved in from the
# static evaluation -- instead of on the static score. A certified lower bound
# above the static score buys a cutoff the node did not have, so the candidate
# prunes more there. On that tree the row "pruning does not hide a forced mate"
# carried for S095 -- 4brbr/p2p1p1p/P2P1P1P/6R1/8/K7/8/1k6 w - - 0 1, depth 11,
# distance 2 -- stopped being true of the shipped build at that one depth: its
# cells for depths 3..12 read
#
#   parent 7c12686   2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2
#   candidate        2 2 2 2 2 2 2 2 - 2
#
# so the mate is found at every swept depth but 11, at the same distance, and
# found again at 12. A golden whose derivation moved is re-derived by its own
# script (DEC-142); S188 did it when its check extension stopped the multicut
# row separating, and S236 did it here, twice, restoring the old row when its
# term left.
#
# WHAT RAN. adocs/data/S095_mine_mate_row.py's own procedure with this
# worktree's paths, kept at .tuning/coord/S234_remine_sweeps.sh and, for the
# shipped sweep, the same command run first by hand:
#   * the candidate set is NOT re-derived -- it is the corpus and the oracle,
#     neither of which moved since S095 mined them, and
#     .tuning/coord/S095_candidates.fen still holds the 141 positions
#   * all sweeps are S230_mine_r01_row.py's `depths`, which compiles a throwaway
#     driver against libchesso_engine.a and calls search() once per depth from a
#     cold 4 MB table -- search_fen()'s own shape, not `go depth`, and the two
#     disagree on exactly this class
#   * the guard is opened textually at the one call site,
#     `const bool no_tt_move = tt_move == 0;` -> `= true;`, rebuilt, swept, and
#     reverted with a sha check (the S033 protocol)
#   * the pick rule is the script header's, applied by the script
#
# THE RESULT. The pick returns **the same position at depth 8**. Only the depth
# literal in the case moves; the position and the distance 2 do not.
#
#   take: 4brbr/p2p1p1p/P2P1P1P/6R1/8/K7/8/1k6 w - - 0 1
#     depth 8, shipped [d3 d4 d5 d6 d7 d8 d9 d10 d12], unguarded loses d8,
#     longest consecutive run 8
#
# The old depth stays in the case's GOLDEN block: an H0 on S234 flips
# RfpTtEstimate to 0, which is the parent's tree to the node, and 11 comes back
# as a revert rather than a second re-mine.
#
# WHAT THE SAME SWEEPS SAY ABOUT THE CANDIDATE MORE BROADLY, recorded because
# it is the number a reader of the paragraph above will want and because it is
# not flattering. Over the whole 141-position candidate set at depths 3 to 12,
# 1410 cells:
#
#   mates reported   parent 497   candidate 489   delta -8
#   rows whose profile differs   29
#   rows reporting fewer mates   16      rows reporting more   9
#
# So the candidate finds eight fewer mate cells than the parent in this cold
# fixed-depth regime, two-sided rather than systematic. A fixed-depth mate count
# is a reading of a larger or smaller tree and not of a better one (DEC-019):
# S188's check extension found 180 against 146 on the S145 set and lost 13.84
# Elo. It is stated here as a cost the SPRT is being asked about, and the
# instruments that are not cold single-depth searches are green -- test_engine's
# "engine: mate safety" over 48 constructed forced mates, test_mate_carry's
# sweep grid, and the engine driven as a GUI drives it: through iterative
# deepening at Hash 16 and at Hash 4 the candidate reports this position's mate
# in 2 at every depth from 3 to 12 with node counts identical to the parent's.
#
# Red observed with the guard opened at the new depth and green with it in
# place: .tuning/coord/S234_observe_red.log.
#
# ==== the pick, as the script printed it ====
take: 4brbr/p2p1p1p/P2P1P1P/6R1/8/K7/8/1k6 w - - 0 1
  depth 8, shipped profile [d3 d4 d5 d6 d7 d8 d9 d10 d12], unguarded [d3 d4 d5 d6 d7 d9 d10 d12], longest consecutive run 8
MINE-DONE

